Using analytic geometry, we find that the center of dilation is at <imath>(-4,-6)</imath> and the coefficient/factor is <imath>1.5</imath>. Then, we see that the origin is <imath>2\sqrt{13}</imath> from the center, and will be <imath>1.5 \times 2\sqrt{13} = 3\sqrt{13}</imath> from it afterwards.

Thus, it will move <imath>3\sqrt{13} - 2\sqrt{13} = \boxed{\sqrt{13}}</imath>.

==Solution 3: Logic and Geometry==

Using the ratios of radii of the circles, <imath>\frac{3}{2}</imath>, we find that the scale factor is <imath>1.5</imath>. If the origin had not moved, this indicates that the center of the
circle would be <imath>(3,3)</imath>, simply because of <imath>(2 \cdot 1.5,  2 \cdot 1.5)</imath>. Since the center has moved from <imath>(3,3)</imath> to <imath>(5,6)</imath>, we apply the distance formula and get: <imath>\sqrt{(6-3)^2 + (5-3)^2} = \boxed{\sqrt{13}}</imath>.

==Solution 4: Simple and Practical==

Start with the size transformation. Transforming the circle from <imath>r=2</imath> to <imath>r=3</imath> would mean the origin point now transforms into the point <imath>(-1,-1)</imath>. Now apply the position shift: <imath>3</imath> to the right and <imath>4</imath> up. This gets you the point <imath>(2,3)</imath>. Now simply apply the Pythagorean theorem with the points <imath>(0,0)</imath> and <imath>(2,3)</imath> to find the requested distance.

==Solution 5: Using the Axes==

Before dilation, notice that the two axes are tangent to the circle with center <imath>(2,2)</imath>. Using this, we can draw new axes tangent to the radius 3 circle with center <imath>(5,6)</imath>, resulting in a "new origin" that is 3 units left and 3 units down from the center <imath>(5,6)</imath>, or <imath>(2,3)</imath>. Using the distance formula, the distance from <imath>(0,0)</imath> and <imath>(2,3)</imath> is <imath>\boxed{\sqrt{13}}</imath>.
~Mightyeagle , ~ RANGANATH12

==Solution 6: Geometry==

Because the dilation changes the circle of radius 2 to a circle of radius 3, the scale factor must be <imath>\frac{3}{2}</imath>. The center of the circle with radius 3 is 3 units to the right and 4 units above the center of the circle with radius 2, so the center of dilation must be 6 units to the left and 8 units below the center of the radius 2 circle. So, the center of dilation is the point <imath>(-4, -6)</imath>. The distance from that point to the origin is <imath>2\sqrt{13}</imath>, and halving that to get the distance the origin moves gives <imath>\boxed{\sqrt{13}}</imath>. ~Meeshbot

==Solution 7: Vectors==

Let \(\mathbf{a}\) and \(\mathbf{a}^{\prime }\) be the position vectors of the circle centers, and let \(\mathbf{c}\) be the position vector of the unknown center of dilation. <cmath>\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}</cmath> <cmath>\mathbf{a}{\prime} = \begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix}.</cmath> The scale factor <imath>k</imath> is the ratio of the final radius to the initial radius, which means <imath>k=\frac{3}{2}</imath>. A dilation scales the displacement vector from the center of dilation <imath>\mathbf{c}</imath> to any point by a factor of \(k\). For the center of the circle, this gives <cmath>\mathbf{a}^{\prime }-\mathbf{c}=k(\mathbf{a}-\mathbf{c})</cmath>. <cmath>\to \mathbf{a}^{\prime }-\mathbf{c}=k\mathbf{a}-k\mathbf{c}</cmath>
<cmath>\to k\mathbf{c}-\mathbf{c}=k\mathbf{a}-\mathbf{a}{\prime}</cmath> 
<cmath> \to \mathbf{c}(k-1)=k\mathbf{a}-\mathbf{a}^{\prime }</cmath> 
<cmath>\to \frac{k\mathbf{a} - \mathbf{a}'}{k-1}</cmath>

Substitute <imath>k = \frac{3}{2}</imath>, <imath>\mathbf{a}</imath>, and <imath>\mathbf{a}^{\prime }</imath> into the formula:
<cmath>\mathbf{c}=\frac{\frac{3}{2}\left(\begin{matrix}2\\ 2\end{matrix}\right)-\left(\begin{matrix}5\\ 6\end{matrix}\right)}{\frac{3}{2}-1}=\frac{\left(\begin{matrix}3\\ 3\end{matrix}\right)-\left(\begin{matrix}5\\ 6\end{matrix}\right)}{\frac{1}{2}}=2\left(\begin{matrix}-2\\ -3\end{matrix}\right)=\left(\begin{matrix}-4\\ -6\end{matrix}\right)</cmath>

Let \(\mathbf{o}\) be the vector of the origin \(\left(\begin{matrix}0\\ 0\end{matrix}\right)\) and \(\mathbf{o}^{\prime }\) be its image. The displacement vector from the origin to its image is \(\mathbf{o'} - \mathbf{o}\). Using the properties of dilation: \(\mathbf{o}^{\prime }-\mathbf{c}=k(\mathbf{o}-\mathbf{c})\). Subtract \(\mathbf{o} - \mathbf{c}\) from both sides to find the displacement \(\mathbf{o'} - \mathbf{o}\):\(\mathbf{o}^{\prime }-\mathbf{o}=(k-1)(\mathbf{o}-\mathbf{c})\). Substitute known values: 

<cmath>\mathbf{o'} - \mathbf{o} = \left(\frac{3}{2} - 1\right) \left( \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} -4 \\ -6 \end{pmatrix} \right)=\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}</cmath>



The distance the origin moves is simply the magnitude of the displacement vector \(\mathbf{o'} - \mathbf{o}\):<cmath>\text{Distance}=\|\mathbf{o}^{\prime }-\mathbf{o}\|=\sqrt{2^{2}+3^{2}}=\sqrt{4+9}=\boxed{(C) \sqrt{13}}.</cmath>
~ erik54321


